const LEAD_DEFAULT="### 1.1 Role and assignment\n\nAct as a senior web developer and designer.\n\n- **Design and develop:** {project}. {brief}\n- **Pages to build:** {pages}\n- **Frameworks:** {frameworks}\n\nBefore you design, list every part of each page in detail: sections, components, content and states.\n\n### 1.2 Sources you must follow\n\n- For design thinking, strictly follow every markdown file on development, design and principles: anti-slop, responsive website, design system and website builder.\n- Reference images (mobile, tablet and desktop screens) are for assessment only. Do not copy them.\n- The attached design system (overview, typography, colour palette, colour values and usage, spacing and grid, elevation and shadow, border and radius) is the source of truth for every visual decision.\n- If I send more files during the build, apply them from that point on without pausing.\n\n### 1.3 How you operate\n\n- Work as a closed loop on every step: Think, Design, Build, Validate, Document, Preserve.\n- Run the whole workflow continuously. Do not stop to ask me questions. Where information is missing, choose the best option for this project, label it \"Suggested:\" and keep going.\n- The one exception is the logo. Present the logo concepts and wait for my choice before you apply a final mark. Until then, use a clearly labelled placeholder mark and keep building.\n- Start every reply with the phase and step you are working on.\n- End the run with an exportable link or file that carries a semantic version number, for example project-name-v1.0.0.html, plus a short changelog.\n\n### 1.4 Project guidelines\n\n1. The design is creative and unique, but not overly complex for developers to build.\n2. Include the components and variants that best practice calls for.\n3. The site is fully functional and feels premium: intuitive, user-friendly and human-centred.\n\n### 1.5 Research, ideation and planning\n\n- Apply the anti-slop system: filter out generic AI-made UI, copy and code so every output is intentional.\n- Follow UI and UX design principles throughout.\n- Use naming that converts cleanly to Figma with the html.to.design plugin.\n\n### 1.6 Naming conventions\n\n| What | Format | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Images and icons | type_location_number | img_nav_01.jpg, icn_header_02.png |\n| CSS classes (static sites only) | BEM | nav_list, nav_list-item, nav_list-item--active |\n| Code components | component based, kebab-case | nav-menu, hero-banner, section-heading |\n| Figma layers (only when translating to Figma) | kebab-case | nav-list-item, nav-list-item-active |\n\n### 1.7 Figma-ready build\n\n- Build components with variants, Auto Layout, shared styles and variables.\n- Assemble every page from reusable components, with close attention to structure, accessibility, responsiveness and component states.\n- The result must convert into a Figma file developers can trust as the source of truth.\n\n### 1.8 Design and content\n\n- Pages look finished and intentional even without final photography or copy. Use clearly labelled placeholders where needed.\n- Work within the existing brand identity, logo system, colour palette and photography direction. Do not introduce unrelated styles.\n- Keep one cohesive visual system across page types, content lengths and layouts.\n- Use real, specific content. No lorem ipsum or dummy text.\n\n### 1.9 Accessibility: WCAG 2.2 {wcag}\n\n- 4.5:1 contrast for normal text, 3:1 for large text and icons.\n- Clearly visible focus states.\n- Tap targets of at least 44 px.\n- Never convey meaning through colour alone.\n- A high-contrast theme and three visitor-controlled text sizes, with every component covered in each.\n- Revise any design that fails an accessibility check or creates usability or performance problems.\n\n### 1.10 Performance and responsive design\n\n- Design for slow connections and older phones: lightweight pages, restrained imagery, no hero video by default, minimal motion.\n- Mobile first, then adapt for tablet, desktop and wide screens.\n- Stay visually strong without heavy imagery, animation or effects.\n\n### 1.11 Components and states\n\nDesign components and their states, not only finished screens. Every component covers:\n\n- Empty, error and loading states\n- Long text and different content lengths\n- Missing images\n\nAlso design:\n\n- Four service availability states\n- Notice modal\n- Vacancy or job feed\n- Search with no results\n- 404 page\n- Breadcrumbs\n- An optional side column used across several page types\n- Six service category icons and a small interface icon set, as SVG files\n\n### 1.12 Handover and production\n\n- SVG logo lockups\n- Exportable icons\n- Clearly named layers\n- Documented spacing system\n- Documented typography and type scale\n- Typefaces licensed for web use, with licence details\n\n### 1.13 Useful extras\n\n- One section needs an illustrated map showing seven locations across a regional area.\n\n### 1.14 Design system\n\n- One centralised system built on primitives, semantic tokens and variables.\n- Detailed and production ready, so it can go straight to a development team as a Figma design system.\n- Common patterns and user-friendly naming.\n\n### 1.15 Design thinking and implementation\n\n- Apply UI and UX laws and principles.\n- Use an 8 pt base grid for all spacing and layout.\n- Meet colour contrast targets everywhere.\n- Use shadows and elevation with purpose.\n- Keep a clear content hierarchy: never stack four text levels in a row.\n- Set type on a baseline grid, with line height and letter spacing derived from the base font. Choose typefaces with a human touch, not default AI picks.\n- Outline the structure of every element and component.\n- Layouts never collapse or overflow.\n- Add icons, motion or graphics only where they support the concept.\n- No blue AI gradients and none of the four stock AI colour tones. Choose a smooth, professional palette that fits the business and its audience.\n\n### 1.16 Design approach and commands\n\nWork as a senior UI/UX designer through: intent discovery, information hierarchy, state completeness, form UX, feedback and affordance, UX audit, design system and visual character.\n\nApply these commands, or their approach where a command is not available:\n\n- /ux-design, /ux-audit, /ux-review, /restyle\n- Impeccable: npx impeccable install, then /impeccable init\n  - Plan: /impeccable shape. A design brief from discovery, not guesswork.\n  - Review: /impeccable critique. Scoring, persona tests and automated detection.\n  - Refine: /impeccable polish. The final pass from good to great.\n  - Iterate: /impeccable live. Comment on an element in the browser, compare three variants, keep one.\n- Taste skill: npx skills add Leonxlnx/taste-skill\n- Emil Kowalski's design skill: npx skills add emilkowalski/skill\n\n### 1.17 Code and design review\n\n- Pass all HTML, CSS and JavaScript validation.\n- SEO: defer scripts, optimise images, autoprefix CSS and JavaScript.\n- Meet Lighthouse, Core Web Vitals, SEO and WCAG best practice.\n- Check performance, accessibility and responsiveness across current browsers.\n\n### 1.18 Tools\n\nHTML5, CSS, JavaScript, jQuery, basic APIs and web security, and JSON where needed.\n\n### 1.19 Logo\n\nFollow the attached logo design brief and process files. This is the only point where you stop and wait for my choice.";
